Subject: virtual-io: keep interest wakers in fixed slots instead of a map

Every poll of every socket goes through InterestWakerMap. A poll clears
the interest it is about to wait on, registers its waker under it and
then tries the socket; the selector thread pushes readiness into the
same map, which wakes the wakers waiting on that interest or, when none
is waiting, records the interest for the next poll to find. Both halves
lived in one Mutex over a HashMap<InterestType, Vec<Waker>> and a
HashSet<InterestType>, so every one of those steps hashed an enum of
four variants and took the same lock: three times per poll on the read
path, four on the write path, and once more per readiness event. In a
profile of a guest under load, the waker map, the selector and the poll
guards together were 9% of the runtime's user-mode samples.

Give InterestType an index and a bit, and keep the two halves in the
shape they describe. The wakers become a fixed array of four vectors
behind their own mutex, reached by index rather than by hash, and each
slot keeps the space it grew to rather than the entry being removed and
rebuilt around every event. The triggered set becomes an AtomicU8 of
the same four bits, so push, pop and has_interest are one atomic
read-modify-write or load each and take no lock at all. What is left on
the poll path is the one lock the waker registration needs.

What the callers rely on is that an event landing while a poll is
registering cannot be missed by both sides. push_interest sets the bit
with the wakers lock held, so a poll whose add took the lock first has
left a waker in the slot for the event to wake, and a poll whose add
takes the lock afterwards sees the bit set by the time add returns;
every caller then either retries the socket, as the read and accept
paths do, or asks has_interest, as the write path does for a closed
peer. Wakers are woken with the lock held, as they were when they lived
in the map behind the same mutex. Off that lock the mask is only ever
changed a bit at a time, so taking one interest cannot disturb another,
and a reader that races a writer sees the mask from one side of the
write or the other, exactly as a read an instant earlier would have.

WakerInterestHandler, which a poll of a socket that has no waker map
builds from the task's waker and drops again, holds its interests in
the same four bits rather than in a HashSet.

Unit tests cover an interest pushed with nothing waiting being left for
the next poll to pop, a waiting waker being woken exactly once with the
interest not also recorded, a waker registered twice waking once and
two distinct wakers both waking, one interest not disturbing another,
and the race itself: five hundred rounds of an event landing on one
thread while a poll registers on another, each asserting that the round
either woke the waker or left the interest to be seen.

